    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d1475">

                <pb facs="1169" n="1159" sameAs="frus:frus1949v08/pg1159"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00/5–3149: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Cabot</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1949-05-31">May 31, 1949.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received May 31—8:09 a. m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1907. <hi rendition="#italic">North China Daily News</hi>, May 31, reports Chang
                    Han-fu, Chief Foreign Affairs Department, SMCC,<anchor n="35" xml:id="d1475-f1"
                    /> announcement that, although Chinese People’s Government has not yet
                    established formal relations with other countries protection foreign lives [and]
                    properties assured under article 8 joint declaration Mao Tse-tung<anchor n="36"
                        xml:id="d1475-f2"/> and Chu <pb facs="1170" n="1160" xml:id="pg1160"/>
                        Teh.<anchor n="37" xml:id="d1475-f3"/> All laws, decrees issued by military
                    political authorities will be published <hi rendition="#italic">Liberation</hi>
                    daily. Questions re travel and residence may be submitted to Public Safety
                    Bureau Police headquarters. Foreigners visiting these offices to bring own
                    interpreters. SMCC regulations governing conduct foreigners state foreigners
                    must obey all laws, not conduct espionage, oppose liberation Chinese people,
                    protect or harbour Chinese war criminals or counter-revolutionist[s].</p>
                <p>Sent Department 1907, repeated Nanking 1042, Canton 583.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Cabot</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d1475-f1" n="35" xml:id="d1475-f1-content">
                <p>Shanghai Military Control Commission (Communist).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1475-f2" n="36" xml:id="d1475-f2-content">
                <p>Chairman of the Central Committee of the Chinese Communist Party (CCP).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d1475-f3" n="37" xml:id="d1475-f3-content">
                <p>Commander in Chief of the Chinese Communist armies.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
